还有一些人，特别是当你进入戒烟领域或酒精治疗领域时，他们不想走进成瘾治疗诊所。他们可能会觉得那对他们来说带有太强的污名化色彩，或者那不是他们可能感兴趣的地方。因此，我认为这绝对是一个正在进行许多令人兴奋的工作的领域。科格林博士，你想要插话。是的，我只想补充一点麦克唐纳博士刚才所说的话。因此，在数字权变管理领域，我们发现非常有趣的一点是，可能存在一个机会来减轻患者参与权变管理的负担。我想说，目前权变管理面临的最大

63
00:34:39.000 --> 00:35:19.000
challenge that's facing contingency management right now is making sure that we really are getting it to people and to the majority of people. For this to have an impact, it can't be a bespoke model that only a very, very few people ever receive. Whether we're talking about smoking, drinking, stimulants, opioids, we really need it to be something that's widely available. There's been so much work done in the addiction space and the mental health space broadly to think how we get evidence-based care into population health settings. And digital is, I'd say, one important tool for that. So we do some work with tobacco use, providing an app-based experience, but making sure that we still have it interfacing with clinicians or coaches, because for many people, as Dr. McDonald did a wonderful job,
挑战是确保我们真正将它提供给人们以及绝大多数人。要产生影响，它不能是一个只有极少数人才能获得的定制模式。无论我们谈论的是吸烟、饮酒、兴奋剂还是阿片类药物，我们真的需要让它成为一种广泛可用的服务。在成瘾领域和更广泛的精神卫生领域，人们已经做了大量工作来思考如何将循证护理引入群体健康设置中。数字化可以说是一个重要的工具。因此，我们围绕烟草使用做了一些工作，提供基于应用程序的体验，但确保它仍然与临床医生或教练进行交互，因为对许多人来说，正如麦克唐纳博士出色地

64
00:35:19.000 --> 00:36:01.000
sort of exemplifying, especially if they're having trouble with initial, not using initially, having positive samples, that relationship, that interaction with the clinician is so key, is so reinforcing, working towards that early success, that high five is really, really important. So my personal thinking on this currently is there is definitely a space for digital contingency management, and we need to be thinking about how that interfaces with what is really important, again, to us as humans and also reinforcing, which is connecting with someone in that sense of belonging. And so I think that these two pieces are really coming together in the science. And also there's some companies out there that are doing really forward-thinking work thinking about
展示的那样，特别是如果他们在最初的戒毒或初始阶段遇到困难、出现阳性样本时，这种关系、与临床医生的这种互动是如此关键，如此具有正向强化作用，朝着早期的成功而努力，那种击掌庆祝真的非常非常重要。因此，我目前对此的个人看法是，数字权变管理绝对有一席之地，我们需要思考它如何与对我们人类而言同样重要且具有正向强化作用的事物相交互，也就是与某人建立联系以及那种归属感。因此，我认为这两部分在科学上真正融合在了一起。此外，还有一些公司正在做极具前瞻性的工作，思考

65
00:36:01.000 --> 00:36:48.000
how we can build models of digital contingency management that both have fidelity to these key mechanisms of contingency management, incentive magnitudes that are meaningful to people, frequency of testing and getting incentives to people right away so they feel that the reward really is counterbalancing the reinforcing effects of the drug, but doing so in a way that is really scalable. And so I think that it's an exciting time in that space because we're really thinking about how can we reach people, whether they live in the UP of Michigan or in Seattle, and make sure that they're getting access across the board to the treatments of work. So I just want to wrap up by asking you both about the biggest challenges in getting CM to all the people who would benefit from it.
我们如何才能建立数字权变管理模型，使其既能忠实于权变管理的核心机制（对人们有意义的奖励幅度、检测频率并立即向人们发放奖励，让他们觉得奖励确实抵消了药物的正向强化效应），又能以真正可扩展的方式进行。因此，我认为这是该领域一个令人兴奋的时期，因为我们真正在思考如何能够触及人们，无论他们住在密歇根州的上半岛还是西雅图，并确保他们能够全面获得起作用的治疗。因此，我最后想问你们两个关于将CM推广给所有能从中受益的人时所面临的最大挑战是什么。

66
00:36:48.000 --> 00:37:22.000
Because what I hear you're saying is it's proven to be effective. We've got the evidence. It's been tested since 2011, you said was when the VA C.M. started working on this, and yet it's not available in every community to all the people who need it. So where are the stumbling blocks? I'll start out, and then Dr. McDonald could have lost word here. So it's multifactor, as it always is. So I'd say we've made incredible progress in the policy space, and we've also made a lot of progress in the payer space. For a long time, the reasons everyone said we didn't have wide enough scale contingency management were that we hadn't figure out
因为我听到你们说它已被证明是有效的。我们有证据。它自2011年以来一直在接受测试——你提到那是退伍军人事务部（VA）开始开展C.M.工作的时间——然而它并没有在每个社区向所有需要它的人提供。那么，绊脚石在哪里呢？我先开始，然后麦克唐纳博士可以做最后的总结。所以，正如以往一样，这是多因素的。因此，我想说我们在政策领域取得了令人难以置信的进展，我们在支付方领域也取得了很大进展。长期以来，每个人都说我们没有足够大规模推广权变管理的理由是，我们还没有弄清楚

67
00:37:22.000 --> 00:38:08.000
how to pay for it at scale, which we're doing right now through demonstration, projects through state funds in many cases. Like we're cracking that nut, which is really incredible. And I'd also say, can you mention the SAMHSA advisory that came out, sort of the federal communication of like we are behind meaningful amounts of incentives to make sure that this is available really helps to alleviate some hesitation that we were seeing from the policy side of getting this out. So that was a really, really big progress. To me, the biggest challenges we're facing is thinking through how we are going to do this across a really varied substance use disorder treatment infrastructure. And it takes, I think, a couple things. One, it takes a lot of focus on
如何在宏观上为之付费——而我们现在通过许多州的示范项目和州基金正在做到这一点。就像我们正在攻克这个难题一样，这真的非常了不起。我还想说，你可以提及美国联邦物质滥用和精神健康服务管理局（SAMHSA）发布的咨询意见，这是一种联邦层面的沟通，表明我们支持提供大量的激励措施以确保其真正可用，这确实有助于缓解我们在推动政策落地时看到的一些迟疑。所以这真的是一个非常非常大的进步。对我来说，我们面临的最大挑战是思考如何在高度多样化的物质使用障碍治疗基础设施中开展这项工作。我认为这需要几点。首先，它需要高度关注

68
00:38:08.000 --> 00:39:04.000
developing our contingency management champions, right? And this means people across all levels. You're talking to Dr. McDonald and I, but there are a lot of people that care deeply about our addiction care, making sure that we are developing people that are going to champion this within clinics, within Department of Health, within our states, within our federal agencies. So that's one real key. I think we're working hard on. The other piece that I focus a lot on is that contingency management is not necessarily accessible to everyone when we do it in clinic. So what we've alluded to a little bit is this idea of coming to a clinic twice a week when we're talking about sort of the biggest place we need contingency management right now is stimulant use disorder. That doesn't work if you have a lot of child care responsibilities. If you live more than 10 miles from a clinic, if you don't have regular access to transportation. And so we really need to be thinking about how we can lower barriers for people to receive contingency management across all sorts of who are living all sorts of different lives.
培养我们的权变管理倡导者，对吧？这意味着各个层面的倡导者。你在和麦克唐纳博士和我对话，但有许多人深深关心我们的成瘾护理，我们需要确保培养出能够在诊所内、卫生部门内、各州以及联邦机构内倡导这一疗法的人。这是关键的一点，我认为我们正在为此努力。我关注的另一个重点是，当我们在诊所进行权变管理时，它并不一定对每个人都容易获得。因此，我们略微提及的一个想法是，当我们需要权变管理的最主要领域是兴奋剂使用障碍时，一周去诊所两次是行不通的。如果你有很多照顾孩子的责任；如果你住在距离诊所10英里以外的地方；如果你没有常规的交通方式，这就行不通。因此，我们真正需要思考的是，如何为生活在各种不同生活环境中的人们降低接受权变管理的障碍。

69
00:39:04.000 --> 00:39:35.000
And so we've thought a lot about this in the opioid use disorder space. And I think we can learn a lot when we think about how to lower barriers to make sure that people that have stimulant use disorders are able to receive contingency management equally. Thank you, Dr. Coggan. I would just add, because I'm sitting in my office here, here, and I remember sitting here six years ago, and I just met with a reporter about, and she asked why isn't contingency management we used outside of the VA? And she just wanted to meet with one client who had a stimulant use disorder who wasn't being
我们在阿片类药物使用障碍领域对此进行了很多思考。我认为当我们思考如何降低障碍以确保兴奋剂使用障碍患者能够平等获得权变管理时，我们可以学到很多东西。谢谢你，科格林博士。我只想补充一点，因为我正坐在我的办公室里，我记得六年前我坐在那里，我刚刚见了一位记者，她问为什么权变管理不在退伍军人事务部（VA）之外使用？她只想见一位患有兴奋剂使用障碍但未得到

70
00:39:35.000 --> 00:40:03.000
served by the VA, and we couldn't, a bunch of us experts that are outside the VA couldn't find a single person. We couldn't find one person that she could talk to. And I'd sat in my office, and I was depressed. I was like, what's the point? I didn't sign up to be a researcher and develop treatments that aren't being used. So I had a personal goal of just trying to figure out why the heck is this going on? Because I've had lots of, until that point, most of the feedback I'd get about why contingency
VA服务患者，而我们做不到，我们这群VA之外的专家找不到哪怕一个人。我们甚至找不到一个她可以交谈的人。我坐在办公室里，感到沮丧。我想，这有什么意义？我当研究人员并开发未被使用的治疗方法可不是为了这个。因此，我当时有一个个人目标，就是想弄清楚这到底是怎么回事？因为在此之前，关于为什么权变

71
00:40:03.000 --> 00:40:24.000
management shouldn't happen or isn't happening is because it was immoral. People shared their moral concerns about it. You know, why are you paying people to stop using drugs? And we'll often see a story about contingency manager with that headline, which is unfortunate. So that's sort of stigma around the intervention. But really, once I started learning about this, I learned it. It was actually not that at all. It was the funding piece.
管理不应该发生或没有发生，我收到的大多数反馈都是因为它是不道德的。人们分享了他们对它的道德担忧。你知道，为什么要付钱让人停止吸毒？我们经常会看到以这个标题报道权变管理的文章，这很不幸。因此，这就是围绕这种干预措施的污名化。但事实上，当我开始了解这方面时，我发现根本不是那么回事。问题出在资金上。

72
00:40:24.000 --> 00:40:42.000
This is not a talk therapy. This is not a medication. So there's really not a practical way to pay for it. And that's why the VA has been so successful because their healthcare system is organized in a different way than the out sort of the rest of our healthcare systems for the most part in the United States. And so you all were able to overcome that.
这不是谈话疗法。这不是药物治疗。因此，确实没有一种切实可行的方法来为之付费。这就是为什么VA如此成功，因为他们的医疗系统在组织方式上与美国其他大部分地区的医疗系统有所不同。因此，你们能够克服这一点。

73
00:40:42.000 --> 00:41:12.000
And then also there's amazing things that you were able to do inside the VA to really fundraise and get leadership support to pay for it. So the changes we mentioned about, the SAMHSA changes are wonderful, and we need to keep working on those funding to have multiple ways to fund this intervention and to have like a billing code for contingency management, which doesn't exist. The other reason, though, and I think the bigger reason, is regulatory. And I absolutely did never want to learn about this. I didn't want to learn about urine testing, like I said, but I also didn't want to learn about regulatory and taxes and things like that.
而且，你们在VA内部还能够做出令人惊叹的事情，进行筹款并获得领导层的支持来为之付费。因此，我们提到的变化——SAMHSA的变化非常棒，我们需要继续致力于这些资金筹措，以拥有多种资助该干预措施的途径，并且拥有一个目前尚不存在的权变管理的计费代码。不过，另一个原因，也是我认为更重要的原因，是监管方面的。我绝对从来不想了解这些。正如我所说，我不想了解尿检，但我同样不想了解监管、税收之类的事情。

74
00:41:12.000 --> 00:41:52.000
So, you know, in general, it's prohibited to give a Medicaid, a patient who's care is funded by Medicaid money. And there's been a lot of fraud outside of the addiction space. There's been a lot of fraud, billions of dollars of fraud over the last 20 years, where providers would provide an incentive like a gift card to somebody to come in for a health screening. And they'd run up all these medically unnecessary tests. And so the federal government has good reasons to be concerned across all administrations that that's happening. And they don't want that to happen. And so until the end of the first Trump administration, there really was a prohibition against contingency management in the funded space outside of the VA. And so what we've really worked hard on, and this is where I'm
所以，总的来说，禁止向医疗补助计划（Medicaid）资助医疗费用的患者提供奖励。在成瘾治疗领域之外发生过很多欺诈行为。在过去20年里，曾发生过大量欺诈行为，涉及数十亿美元，当时医疗服务提供者向某人提供诸如礼品卡之类的奖励，吸引他们前来做健康筛查。然后他们进行了所有这些医学上不必要的检查。因此，联邦政府在各个行政部门都有充分的理由担心这种情况发生，他们不希望这种情况发生。因此，直到第一届特朗普政府结束之前，在VA之外的受资助领域，确实存在对权变管理的禁令。所以我们真正努力去做的事情——这也是我作为心理学家为我们感到

75
00:41:52.000 --> 00:42:37.000
really proud of us as psychologists, is that the team who's really, who's led this work, really led by Dr. Rick Rosson and many others, are all, and there are many allied professionals part of this, but really it's been a psychologist-led effort to advocate for the funding and policy reforms that are needed. And so we're at a point now where the demonstration, Medicaid demonstration waygivers that Dr. Coggin was mentioning really offer one way to get that regulatory approval. And There's other rules that we're learning more about and we're hoping that we get more clarity from the federal government on those rules. But really that regulatory barrier is still our biggest challenge in terms of people really do not want to be subject to Medicaid fraud investigation. The word fraud, waste, and abuse is terrifying to people.
无比自豪的地方，是由里克·罗森（Rick Rosson）博士及许多其他人真正领导的团队推动了这项工作，当然还有许多联合专业人员参与其中，但这确实是一项由心理学家主导的努力，旨在倡导所需的资金和政策改革。因此，我们现在处于这样一个阶段：科格林博士提到的Medicaid示范豁免确实提供了一种获得监管批准的途径。我们还在了解其他一些规则，并希望从联邦政府获得关于这些规则的更多清晰信息。但实际上，就人们真的不想成为Medicaid欺诈调查的目标而言，监管障碍仍然是我们最大的挑战。欺诈、浪费和滥用（fraud, waste, and abuse）这个词对人们来说太可怕了。

